  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 407 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nCurrently through this evening...\n\nAs expected, been a more pleasant afternoon across the region\ntoday. Looking aloft...upper air and satellite data showing a \nwell amplified pattern across the CONUS, with continued \nnorthwesterly flow in place across the Central Plains...sitting \nbetween ridging extending from the Srn Plains northwestward into\nthe Pac NW and troughing running from the Hudson Bay area \nsouthward toward the SE Coast. At the surface, the cold front \nwhich pushed south through the area Mon-Mon night lies generally\nalong the KS/OK border...bringing mid-afternoon temperatures \ntoday in the mid 80s-near 90, vs the highs around 100 we had on \nMonday. In addition to the cooler temps, dewpoints are roughly \n10 degrees lower...currently sitting in the mid 50s to low 60s \nfor most. \n\nHad a narrow swath of isolated showers and storms gradually work \ntheir way south across portions of south central NE last night \ninto this morning. This activity gradually waned with \ntime...with this afternoon being dry. \n\nExpecting little change as we get into the evening hours...dry \nconditions continue, with sfc high pressure sliding off to our\neast, bringing more of an easterly component to the overall \nlight winds.\n\nLate tonight through Thursday night...\n\nThis mid-week period continues to be the most active of this\nentire 7-day forecast period, with periodic upper levels \ndisturbances bringing the potential for much-welcome rainfall. \nAs nice as it would be to say this will be a period of non-stop,\ngentle rain...this will be coming in waves, with potentially \nheavy rates at times. \n\nExpecting little change overall in the upper level northwesterly \nflow tonight into Wednesday...with the first of these shortwave \nupper level disturbances looking to start working its way in \nmainly after midnight. Models showing this wave not being an \noverly strong one...with some runs showing QPF potentially more \nscattered in nature, though most focus the better chances \nroughly across the NNErn half of the forecast area. If anything \nmodels have trended back on how long through the day activity \nlasts...showing the brunt potentially over around midday or so, \nleaving little else but some lingering isolated precip around \nthe rest of the afternoon. \n\nAfter that lull...models showing the potential for another\ncouple of shortwave disturbances to push through the region. \nThe timing is not too unlike the first...mainly affecting the \nevening/overnight-early morning hours Wed night-Thu AM and Thu \nnight-Fri AM, with the potential for a relative lull during a \nchunk of the daytime hours. Models showing that these \ndisturbances are stronger, having better potential to dig a bit \nfurther south into the ridging, allowing it to track more over \nthe heart of the area vs riding around the NErn edge. \n\nExpecting some thunderstorms embedded in this mid-week \nactivity...and while deeper layer isn't bad, models are in\npretty good agreement showing that instability is \nlacking...keeping the overall threat for severe weather during \nthis period on the lower side. With more southerly flow \nreturning with time, expecting better moisture to gradually \nreturn, esp. for those Wed night-early Fri AM chances...bringing\nincreasing PWAT values and potential for heavier rainfall \nrates. Outside of the GFS/GEFS, most other models showing good \npotential for a decent swath of at least 0.5\" of rain by the \ntime it's all said and done...mainly across the central-NErn \nportions of the forecast area. ECMWF ensembles even continue to \nhave fairly high probabilities of 1\" or more (70+ \npercent)...again mostly driven by that Wed night-Fri AM period. \nWill see how upcoming model runs trend with the finer details.\n\nFriday and on...\n\nFor the end of the week on through the upcoming weekend and\nstart of the new week...models continue to show that upper \nlevel ridging reamplifying (to varying degrees depending on the \nmodel), drying the period back out. Temperatures also climb back\nup...after highs Wed- Thu in the 70s-80s, the heat returns by \nthis weekend-Mon with highs back well into the 90s-near \n100...including heat index values near 105.",
